DJ DeLorie (sp?) wrote djgpp to compile (more or
less, and would probably murder me for the
inaccuracies here) 32 bit programs in a way that let
them run, sort of, under MS-DOS, which, if you
remember, started out with a 640Kbyte address space
and eventually topped out with a 1 Mbyte one, but
had lots of "add-on" OS-extender products that
worked around that limitation somehow as desktop
computer memory got cheaper and memories bigger, but
were still "MS-DOS compatible".

Since the goal of djgpp is to compile programs that
can address larger code spaces and (more or less
coincidentally) larger in-memory data structures, it
is at least believable that djgpp is able to compile
a tiles-equipped NetHack under its vastly beefed up
modifications to how its compiled output works under
MS-DOS.

I'm guessing, since I don't use the NetHack ascii
version, any more, that the tiles plug straight in
as character replacements, but the rest of the
"wrapper" is still the old non-Windows
look-and-feel. It isn't way out of line to guess
that such a version would work if it only had data
pointers big enough to span an array the size of one
needed to hold the tiles data, exactly what djgpp
specializes in providing.

I'm also guessing, since I maintained one as my day
job for 2.5 years after it made any sense not to
have converted it to Unix, that there may _still_ be
legacy MS-DOS software so important, and so hard to
port or rewrite/replace, in various places that
MS-DOS is still a "live" operating system despite
the vendor's opinion to the contrary.

[If you'd _meant_ to compile an MS-Windows version,
two free software development environments that
might work are the Mingw one (with a relatively
small hard disk footprint, since all it provides is
the bare tools to make MS-Windows compiles work more
or less like they do under Unix) and the Cygwin one
(with a fairly huge hard disk footprint, because it
is also a pretty complete emulation of the standard
Unix open source toolkit atop MS-Windows). I'm pretty
sure each provides a "best effort" POSIX-compliant
library kit for C/C++. Googling will lead you to
them both.]

